course: dictionary
lesson: 15496
title: Stung: a sting, a burn, or a hurt
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. «A bee stung my arm at the picnic.» Here stung means hurt by an insect pushing its sharp point into the skin. The word «bee» is the clue.
explain: «The soap stung my eyes.» There is no insect here. The clue is the eyes, and stung means a sharp, burning pain. Now read «Her unkind words stung him.» Words cannot push into skin, so stung means hurt in the feelings.
explain: Stung is the past form of sting. Today a bee stings me; yesterday a bee stung me. Check the meaning by swapping in another word, such as hurt or burned, and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Sting is the present form. Stung is the past form, used for something that happened before now.
fact: «Stung» can mean hurt by an insect, plant or animal that pushes a sharp point or poison into the skin.
fact: «Stung» can mean felt a sharp, burning pain, as in «the soap stung my eyes».
fact: «Stung» can mean upset or hurt in your feelings by unkind words, as in «stung by her comment».
exercise: «A bee stung my arm at the picnic.» What does stung mean here? | Fed by the family | Hugged tightly | *Hurt by a bee pushing its sharp point into the skin | Covered in sticky jam
exercise: «The soap stung my eyes.» What kind of pain is this? | *A sharp, burning pain | A dull, sleepy feeling | A cold, wet feeling | A soft, tickly pain
exercise: «Her unkind words stung him.» What does stung mean here? | Made him laugh | Cooked for a long time | *Hurt in his feelings | Pushed gently
test: Which word is the past form of sting? | sting | *stung | stings | stinging
test: «Her cruel comment stung him, and he went quiet.» What does the quiet suggest? | *He was upset and hurt | He was very happy | He was fast asleep | He was asking for a drink
exam: Which sentence uses stung to mean a sharp, burning pain? | *The lemon juice stung the cut on my finger | The lemon juice stung me to sleep | The lemon juice stung a sunny day
